Subject: [PATCH] r8152: use 16 KiB rx buffers for RTL8156 below SuperSpeed

rtl8152_open() allocates RTL8152_MAX_RX receive aggregation buffers of
rx_buf_sz each. For the RTL8156 family that is 48 KiB, an order-4
allocation, above PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER: on a small system with
fragmented memory it fails quickly, open returns -ENOMEM and the link
never comes up. Behind a USB 2.0 host the larger aggregation buffer
brings nothing; the RTL8153 already uses 16 KiB below SuperSpeed.
Do the same for the RTL8156.

Found on the Powkiddy A12 (RK3128, 256 MiB, EHCI only) with an RTL8156:
on the first boot after a system update NetworkManager's link-up failed
with "Cannot allocate memory" and eth0 stayed without carrier until the
adapter was replugged (TEST_LOG test 57); fragmenting memory reproduces
it on demand.

--- a/drivers/net/usb/r8152.c
+++ b/drivers/net/usb/r8152.c
@@ -9573,7 +9573,10 @@
 		ops->hw_phy_cfg		= r8156_hw_phy_cfg;
 		ops->autosuspend_en	= rtl8156_runtime_enable;
 		ops->change_mtu		= rtl8156_change_mtu;
-		tp->rx_buf_sz		= 48 * 1024;
+		if (tp->udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+			tp->rx_buf_sz	= 16 * 1024;
+		else
+			tp->rx_buf_sz	= 48 * 1024;
 		tp->support_2500full	= 1;
 		r8152_desc_init(tp);
 		break;
@@ -9598,7 +9601,10 @@
 		ops->hw_phy_cfg		= r8156b_hw_phy_cfg;
 		ops->autosuspend_en	= rtl8156_runtime_enable;
 		ops->change_mtu		= rtl8156_change_mtu;
-		tp->rx_buf_sz		= 48 * 1024;
+		if (tp->udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+			tp->rx_buf_sz	= 16 * 1024;
+		else
+			tp->rx_buf_sz	= 48 * 1024;
 		r8152_desc_init(tp);
 		break;
 
